few games use live-action cutscenes as  emphatically as quantum break and for  all intents and purposes it's one of the  games defining elements between each of  its five gameplay based chapters you  have the opportunity to watch roughly 20  minute long episodes which are entirely  optional but designed to offer a wealth  of insight into the antagonist state of  mind otherwise quantum break focuses on  Jack Joyce the time-bending lead  character played by shawn ashmore better  known as Iceman from the x-men films  Ashmore is one of a few well-known  actors featured in quantum break and  each member's professional acting chops  are put to good use of course quantum  break is intended to be played rather  than merely watched but even if you skip  its optional episodes the best aspects  of its gameplay are suppressed by  frequent bouts of mundane indie combat  is the unequivocal star of the show here  driven by time-based super powers at  your disposal at the start of the game  you're imbued with the ability to  manipulate time which allows you to slow  down combat speed up Jack's movement  create shields that freeze bullets upon  impact or cast time blast bombs near  unsuspecting enemies and it doesn't take  long to develop a flow to understand how  to link one ability to the next by  managing each moves cooldown timer you  can thrust yourself into danger to  establish line-of-sight on an enemy and  protect yourself for the shield while  you open fire when your defenses run out  you can quickly zip into cover and cast  a time blast or continue to use your  firearms while your abilities refresh  there's no one right way to fight and  there's great enjoyment to be found in  experimenting with your abilities  not to mention witnessing the  environment react to your powers as the  scene shifts cracks and glitches out  around you quantum break is a great  looking game with some truly impressive  character models that bring its actors  to life and the time based special  effects are a constant source of  amazement there's also an incredible  amount of real-time destruction on  display which not only looks great but  also keeps you moving during combat as  enemies regularly destroy your hiding  spots quantum break shooting mechanics  however are slightly less impressive  there are times when your enemies  introduce devices that disrupt your  superhuman abilities and you have no  choice but to resort to your firearms  now we would be unfair to compare simple  shooting to manipulating time but it is  reasonable to hold quantum break to  modern third-person shooting standards  you spend a lot of time in cover during  combat and while it's possible to blind  fire your time blast ability you can't  use your guns with the same amount of  freedom you don't have the option to  shoot while crouched you have to stand  up and expose yourself to other enemies  and usually take damage unless you have  enough energy to cast a time shield  around yourself of course this is an  option that shouldn't be needed we  already have suitable cover both aiming  and movement feel loose and cumbersome  making the aim-assist feature the only  reliable way to consistently pick off  enemies when you're not engaged in  combat you're probably exploring  environments for clues and story tidbits  or traversing obstacles during  ill-conceived platforming challenges  again movement is so loose in quantum  break and the combats so exciting that  it's frustrating when you're forced to  slow down awkwardly scale objects and  leap around  sadly quantum breaks gameplay is  essentially divided equally between  combat and hapless environmental  traversal given that the entire campaign  can be completed in less than 10 hours  live action episodes included the  distribution of activities feels that  much more disappointing prior to the TV  show like episodes you get to make  decisions for your nemesis Paul serene  which have an impact on some events in  the show and in the game the effect of  most decisions are noticeable but not  truly consequential in part because the  game's cliched potentially story  spoiling opening dulls your sense of  wonder from the get-go far worse however  is the end result quantum break only has  one ending realizing this your choices  ultimately feel trivial and not worth  reconsidering you're encouraged to  replay the game but by the time you  reach the credits it's unlikely you'll  feel motivated to do so so by the end  you know the most important parts of the  story and you understand that replaying  it to witness alternative events in the  middle would require you to retread  well-worn  sometimes boring territory getting to  the end of quantum break can be an  interesting ride at times but no matter  how impressive the combat is no matter  how great the game looks there's no  getting around the fact that it's driven  by a story with limited appeal and  hindered by disappointing design  decisions
 

